In ABH, Advocates replace insurance companies as patient agents managing care. Their business model starts with bidding a fixed (valued at 100%) price (for operating expenses) and a bonus valued at the expectation value (given 5 tiers with the top earning 100%, second 75%, third 50%, fourth 25%, fifth none and the region rebid, the expectation value is bonus/5 *(1+3/4+2/4+1/4+0)=bonus/2) in a reverse Vickrey auction on regions that contain enough people for the care signal to outweigh the noise (guessing 10k or so). On regions won, the Advocates become responsible for the health of people there. They may apply copays as needed for their business model (especially moral hazard), but if copays decrease care, health may suffer. At month 11 of the year, the care gets rated by a collection of metrics with weights known 6 months before the year started and government assigns bonuses and unhealthy regions get rebid. In healthy regions, the advocate may choose to extend under the same terms adjusted to CPI without bid. Advocates must make patient data available to Auction Houses. Failure to share required data and improper reporting may result in bonus or region loss. Advocates may spend funds in whatever way they see fit including building clinics, outsourcing to telehealth, ameliorating food deserts, providing recreational opportunity, etc... Clever advocates will bid high fixed and low bonus on unhealthy regions and a low fixed and high bonus for healthy regions. Any advocate who loses more than 1/5th of regions in a year may not bid during the next year. Boutique advocates (Single-region with ownership and staff within the community, supported by SBA) may apply for a four year waiver from re-bid to improve continuity of care while repairing past inequity. Model uses churn and metrics to continually improve health in every region while bidding mechanism applies downward pressure on prices. Government levies significant taxes and makes payments to advocates both for their operating budgets and bonuses. Government also unilaterally (with advice from publicly accountable lawmakers and SMEs) builds metrics and weights with three goals. First, they must operate within a fixed (adjusted for inflation) annual budget on data they can reasonably get - in the early years that will be government data like mortality, disability, unhoused rates and employment information. Second, the weights should provide a demand signal that moderates the massive shift in spending as the US healthcare economy shrinks to a more reasonable size. Third, and most important, the metrics should improve every year at helping people live longer and healthier lives. Government purchases research from Auction Houses both for the purposes of assigning bonuses as well as exploring the cost of new metrics. Auction Houses (hopefully at least 20 in the US) hold secret bids from Advocates as well as providing access to existing balkanized healthcare data through a new distributed database that uses a voting communications protocol (no names or personally identifying data). They serve as a two-sided marketplace, differentiating in how they split prices, accepting requests for research, re-packaging them to hide the requestor's identity (thereby mitigating collusive influences), auctioning those requests off to researchers and then accepting at least two bids (which may become 3 or 4 or 5 or... if they don't match and can't resolve differences). They may then (depending on the terms of the initial bid) repackage and resell (or give away) those results to interested parties. Auction Houses may also commission themselves to perform research, but metric research must be validated by independent researchers. Auction Houses may also provide patients with a procedure to independently acquire their entire medical history. Preferential pricing is outlawed in this system. Patients have care which they likely won't need to pay much (beyond taxes) for. Advocates provide market and care success rate information to patients which improves the gilded care affluent patients currently receive. The investment in measuring population health provides infrastructure to healthcare product researchers, thereby reducing the cost to market for drugs and treatments. It also vastly increases our knowledge about effective treatments and healthy lifestyles. The metric weights of ABH blunt the economic shock of moving from 18% to 12% or even 10%of GDP on healthcare by increasing the duration of the change and providing avenues for impacted parties to pursue new, related fields. As our ability to perform research increases, the cost of building regional metrics diminishes and bonus metrics become more comprehensive. These comprehensive metrics should include items to decrease disparities in care between different populations. Recommend giving 20 years of bonus metric research budget continuity (CPI increases) before allowing budget to shrink. I'd like to discuss faithless advocates - organizations who decide its in their financial interest to either charge exorbitant copays or stop providing care all together knowing that health metrics change over longer time scales than a year.
